---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/atoms/TableScroll.astro'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Settings files', href: '#files' },
  { label: 'Agent executables', href: '#agents' },
  { label: 'Launch environment', href: '#environment' },
  { label: 'Workspace limits', href: '#limits' },
  { label: 'Preview URL', href: '#preview' },
  { label: 'Appearance and keys', href: '#appearance' },
  { label: 'Data and logs', href: '#data' },
]

const settingsExample = `{
  "version": 1,
  "app": {
    "theme": "dark",
    "notifications.enabled": true,
    "terminal.scrollback_lines": 10000,
    "attachments.max_file_bytes": 50000000,
    "agents.opencode.executable": "/etc/profiles/per-user/me/bin/opencode",
    "agents.opencode.config_file": "/Users/me/.config/opencode/opencode.json"
  },
  "projects": {
    "/Users/me/src/example": {
      "terminal.scrollback_lines": 20000,
      "attachments.max_file_bytes": 20000000
    }
  }
}`

const envExample = `{
  "version": 1,
  "app": {
    "agents.codex.environment": {
      "EXAMPLE_TOKEN": "!cat .local-token"
    }
  }
}`

const paths = [
  { platform: 'macOS', path: '~/Library/Application Support/dev.shepherd.shell/settings.json' },
  { platform: 'Linux', path: '$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dev.shepherd.shell/settings.json (default ~/.config/...)' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Configuration"
  description="Where Shepherd's settings live, how project overrides resolve, and how to point it at installed agents."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Nothing here is required to open a project">
    Agent executables and authentication come from the CLIs you already use. Shepherd stores no
    provider keys or secret values.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="files">Settings files</h2>
  <p>
    <code>settings.json</code> and <code>keybindings.json</code> live in the app config directory and
    are the only source of truth for user settings. They are versioned JSON, written atomically with
    owner-only permissions; a newer version refuses edits instead of downgrading.
  </p>
  <TableScroll label="Settings file locations">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Path</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {paths.map((entry) => <tr><td>{entry.platform}</td><td><code>{entry.path}</code></td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The file has an <code>app</code> scope and a <code>projects</code> scope keyed by absolute path.
    Resolution is per key: project, then app, then the built-in default. Values are replaced whole;
    there is no deep merge. A moved project loses its overrides.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="settings.json" code={settingsExample} />
  <p>
    Unknown keys are preserved. A missing file means defaults; a malformed file is copied to
    <code>.bak</code>, defaults load, and a banner names the path.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agents">Agent executables</h2>
  <p>
    Shepherd discovers installed agents, but you can pin an existing executable by absolute path.
    A stable profile symlink is fine. OpenCode can also take an explicit configuration file, passed
    through as <code>OPENCODE_CONFIG</code> for native, ACP and project-mode discovery.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>agents.opencode.executable</code> — absolute path to an existing OpenCode executable.</li>
    <li><code>agents.opencode.config_file</code> — optional absolute path to its configuration file.</li>
    <li><code>agents.claude.executable</code> — absolute path to a Claude Code ACP executable.</li>
    <li><code>agents.claude.args</code> — optional array of arguments passed in order.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Restart Shepherd after changing these. The task keeps its agent after the first prompt; the
    conversation header shows the selected tier, and diagnostics show the command, protocol and
    recent stderr.
  </p>

  <h2 id="environment">Launch environment</h2>
  <p>
    Agent launches accept <code>agents.codex.environment</code>,
    <code>agents.opencode.environment</code> and <code>agents.claude.environment</code> maps. A
    project entry can replace the whole map for that agent.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="settings.json" code={envExample} />
  <p>
    A value beginning with <code>!</code> runs as a shell command from the project root when a
    session starts; its trimmed output is passed to the agent and never saved. Commands time out
    after five seconds. The failure category remains visible, but adapter stderr and detailed launch
    errors are hidden because an agent may print a secret there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Workspace limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>attachments.max_file_bytes</code> — default 50,000,000 bytes per file. Applies to composer files and preview screenshots; a project can override it.</li>
    <li><code>terminal.scrollback_lines</code> — 0 to 100,000, default 10,000. Read when a terminal starts or restarts.</li>
    <li><code>notifications.enabled</code> — opt-in system notifications for waits outside the focused task.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Settings → Workspace edits the defaults; Settings → Projects edits the selected project's
    overrides and resets them. Reload settings re-reads a file edited outside the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="preview">Preview URL</h2>
  <p>
    Save a loopback HTTP(S) address in Settings → Projects. Leaving the field empty clears it. The
    Preview tab loads that address in an isolated surface, and the annotation wrapper embeds it
    without proxying it. Only loopback targets are accepted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pages that refuse framing, block script injection, navigate off the configured origin or are not
    HTML fall back to screenshot annotations. See <a href="/docs/users/preview">Preview and annotation</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="appearance">Appearance and keys</h2>
  <p>
    Settings → Appearance offers System, Light and Dark, compact or comfortable density, and the
    Send accent. Settings → Keyboard shortcuts records one chord per command, rejects conflicts and
    can reset to the platform default. Bindings are app-scoped and live in
    <code>keybindings.json</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the keybindings file cannot be read, Shepherd keeps the default registry and reports the path;
    repair the file and use Reload configuration. A newer file version stays intact.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">Data and logs</h2>
  <p>
    Development runs use <code>.cache/test-db</code>; <code>SHEPHERD_DEV_DATA_DIR</code> overrides
    it. Structured errors are appended to <code>logs/errors.jsonl</code> in the active data directory
    (normally <code>.cache/test-db/logs/errors.jsonl</code>). Error history in the status bar shows
    the same records with their operation and timestamp.
  </p>
  <p>
    Projects, tasks, transcripts, worktrees and settings stay on the machine. There is no account,
    telemetry or crash-report upload.
  </p>
</Docs>
